THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
The proposal requires selecting the first grounded extension ordered by a polynomial-time computable lexicographic priority. But enumerating ALL grounded extensions to then pick the first is itself exponential in general — there can be exponentially many complete extensions of an argumentation framework (a classic counting result). The 'polynomial-time computable priority' only orders extensions that have already been generated; it does not bound the number of extensions. Thus polynomial cost is not actually preserved, violating the problem's required guarantee — and the target itself concedes 'polynomial cost only holds if the number of extensions is polynomially bounded,' which is not generally true.

TARGET:
When the standard grounded operator is not confluent under an enrichment, do not compute a least fixed point; instead order all grounded extensions by a polynomial-time computable lexicographic priority on committed roots and select the first. This strengthens the semantics by making choices explicit while preserving determinism and validity of committed roots. Criticizable because it abandons skeptical agreement and reintroduces a choice principle, so reinstatement is no longer derived but selected, and polynomial cost only holds if the number of extensions is polynomially bounded.